{"compressedText":"# Field Guide: Preparing a Home for a 72-Hour Power Outage\n\nReady an ordinary household to manage safely and comfortably through an outage up to 72 hours. Assumes a temperate climate, no backup generator. Do the sections in order; earlier ones matter most.\n\n## Water\n- Store 4 litres per person per day = 12 litres per person for 72 hours: half for drinking, half for cooking and basic hygiene.\n- Food-grade containers, out of direct sunlight; replace every 6 months.\n- Storm forecast with warning: also fill the bathtub, for flushing toilets and washing, never drinking.\n\n## Food\n- Dedicated shelf of no-cook non-perishable food for 3 days: canned beans, canned fish, peanut butter, crackers, dried fruit, shelf-stable milk.\n- Own a manual can opener; an electric one is useless.\n- Eat in order: perishable fridge food, then freezer, then the emergency shelf.\n\n## Refrigerator and freezer\n- Safe while unopened: fridge about 4 hours; full freezer about 48 hours; half-full freezer about 24 hours.\n- So keep the freezer consolidated and full, filling spare space with containers of frozen water. Keep both doors closed as much as possible; tape a reminder note to each.\n- Discard perishable food over 2 hours above 4 degrees Celsius; when in doubt, throw it out.\n\n## Light\n- Primary light: battery-powered LED lanterns or headlamps. Avoid candles entirely, a leading cause of house fires during outages.\n- At least one light per person plus two spare battery sets each; one headlamp by each bed, one lantern in the kitchen, placed to be found in the dark.\n\n## Heat and safety\n- Never run a camping stove, barbecue, or generator indoors, in a garage, or near a window: each produces carbon monoxide, which is odourless and can be fatal.\n- Install at least one battery-powered carbon monoxide alarm on each floor where people sleep.\n- Cold weather: gather everyone in one small room on a south-facing side, door closed; layered clothing and blankets are safer than any improvised heater.\n- If the indoor temperature falls below 10 degrees Celsius and you cannot maintain warmth, relocate to a warming centre or a neighbour's home.\n\n## Communication and information\n- Battery-powered or hand-crank radio for emergency broadcasts; know your local emergency station's frequency before the outage.\n- When a storm is forecast, charge every phone and power bank: a full 20,000 milliamp-hour bank recharges a typical phone roughly 4 times. At the outage's start, set phones to low-power mode.\n- Pre-agree one out-of-area contact whom every household member texts, because texts get through congested networks more reliably than voice calls.\n\n## Medical needs\n- First-aid kit plus a 7-day supply of every prescription medication; rotate them so they stay in date.\n- Refrigerated medication such as insulin generally stays usable at room temperature for up to 28 days, but confirm your specific medication with a pharmacist beforehand.\n- Write your pharmacy, doctor, and poison-control numbers on paper, because a dead phone is not a phone book.\n\n## When the power returns\n- Wait 5 minutes before switching major appliances back on, so the grid can stabilise.\n- Check the freezer: food that still contains ice crystals can be safely refrozen.\n- Restock everything used within one week, starting with water and batteries.\n- Write a one-page note about what was missing or awkward, and fix at least one of those gaps before the next outage.","expectedMetric":570,"hardConstraintNote":"Still a self-contained instruction document: it keeps the title, all eight named sections in original order, the opening scope statement (ordinary household, safely and comfortably, up to 72 hours, temperate climate, no backup generator, work sections in order, earlier matter most), and every actionable instruction with its numbers. A reader with only this text can execute the full preparation and the recovery steps without reference to the original; nothing was replaced by a pointer, cross-reference, or external dependency.","killedLeverCitations":[],"lens":"restructurer","leverId":"restructure-to-bulleted-sections","rationale":"Structure-level pass: the original is eight paragraphs of connective prose carrying ~40 discrete instruction-facts. I kept all eight sections and their headings but converted each paragraph into 3-4 telegram-style bullets, folded the intro's two framing sentences into one, and merged the freezer hold-times (fridge ~4h / full freezer ~48h / half-full ~24h) into a single comparative line. Symbolic substitutions that lose no meaning: 'seventy-two hours'->'72 hours', 'four litres'->'4 litres', 'twenty thousand milliamp-hours'->'20,000 milliamp-hour', 'twenty-eight days'->'28 days', 'ten degrees Celsius'->'10 degrees Celsius', 'five minutes'->'5 minutes', 'seven-day'->'7-day'. Every quantity, item, temperature, duration and ordered step is retained; only redundant connectives ('which means', 'and only then', 'because each of these', 'before an emergency happens') were dropped. No KILLED_LEVERS entries exist yet (table empty), so no K-id fence applies. Result 570 words vs baseline 730 (GR-1: frontier.json), about -22%, inside the restructurer ceiling band and deliberately short of the ~470 aggressive bound to avoid shedding a gate-relevant fact.","title":"Restructure prose into a bulleted section grid, collapse preamble and connective tissue"}